 A failed I/R/P/O action throws before it appends history. Failed requests are retained by the external cooperation transcript, not inserted as successful browser history.
 
 This patch does not claim broad authorization hardening, all-field schema validation, cryptographic authenticity, or sender-origin authorization. The existing server-side postMessage origin policy is unchanged.
+
+## Local cooperation successor: history snapshot contract
+
+Version `1.2.0-coop.2` extends the prior local candidate. This is not a
+statement of deployment or production adoption.
+
+`irpo` snapshots I/R/P/O using structured cloning and prepares all four
+JSON-rendered fields before committing the record. Later changes to caller-owned
+input, returned receipts, or `history()` copies do not rewrite that snapshot.
+
+A clone or rendering failure returns `INVALID_IRPO_RECORD` without appending
+history or partially changing the I/R/P/O view. Functions cannot be cloned;
+circular structures and BigInt values are not accepted by this JSON-rendered
+interface. The postMessage transport itself cannot carry functions.
+
+SharedArrayBuffer, shared-backed views, and unreviewed host objects are rejected
+recursively, including within Map, Set and Error causes. Ordinary records,
+arrays, detached-copy ArrayBuffer/views, Date, RegExp, Map, Set and Error values
+are permitted only if the full record also passes cloning and JSON rendering.
+Their native JSON display conventions are unchanged; JSON rendering is not
+a promise of a reversible serialization for each of these richer types.
+
+The history is a **rolling in-memory window of at most 512 accepted records**,
+not a persistent or unbounded append-only archive. Rejected requests belong in
+an external transcript. Historical source, caller authorship, and a companion
+receipt remain distinct; carrying a supplied result does not validate its truth.
+
+Boundary: this covers record snapshot and clone/render preparation failures in
+the supplied application. It does not claim transactional guarantees against
+malicious host DOM overrides, independently authorized senders, arbitrary
+side-effectful getters, or a durable audit database.
